{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>The vision.</b> The nun of חזון is added." "<b>[son of Amotz].</b> And there is in the words of an individual [commentator] that every prophet whose father's name is mentioned — he too is a prophet. And there are those who say that he was of royal stock, for Amotz and Amatzyah were brothers; therefore the wicked of Israel could not harm him as they harmed Jeremiah. Now, it is written: \"I gave my back to smiters\" (Isaiah 50:6). And according to the plain sense, every prophet whose father's name is mentioned is great, and his sod is known in his generation; and of them, some are prophets and some are not prophets, as in \"The saying of David son of Yishai\" (2 Samuel 23:1) — and behold, \"David the man of Elohim\" (2 Chronicles 8:14). And when it is said \"Yehu son of Nimshi\" (1 Kings 19:16), Nimshi is not a king. Now, we have found in the vision of Isaiah son of Amotz the prophet (2 Chronicles 32:32), and it is possible that the word \"the prophet\" reverts to Isaiah or that it is attached to Amotz. And since we have found: \"to Isaiah the prophet son of Amotz\" (2 Kings 19:2), we know that he is the prophet. And so Zephaniah mentioned his ancestors (Zephaniah 1:1) on account of Hezekiah." "And the meaning of <b>which he saw [concerning Judah]</b> is that most of his prophecies are about the cities of Judah — that the king of Assyria would seize them — and about Jerusalem, which escaped his hand; and so from the middle of the book, about the exile of Judah, for he did not mention the other tribes." "And his prophecy was <b>in the days of Uzziah.</b> And what seems right to me is that in the year of his death he began to prophesy, as I will yet explain. And according to the plain sense, he died in the days of Hezekiah; for if his days had extended to the days of Manasseh, this would have been written. And there is in the words of an individual [commentator] that Manasseh killed him, because he said, \"My eyes have seen\" (Isaiah 6:5), and if the matter is received tradition, it is the truth."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {1 "Resolved היחיד as 'an individual [commentator]' following the standard medieval usage referring to a specific unidentified exegete.", 3 "Resolved היחיד as 'an individual [commentator]' as in comment 1."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:51:29.834545398Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}